<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: Choose Your Movement Method</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You read this table in <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a> as a <em>prediction</em>. Now it is a
        decision you have to live with for the rest of the year, so read the last two columns
        very carefully — and read the note underneath about the documentation being wrong,
        because someone has already fallen into it for you:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th><th>Stops at walls?</th><th>What it requires</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td>
            <td>Offsets coordinates directly. Never consults physics.</td>
            <td><strong>No</strong></td>
            <td>Nothing. This is what you have now.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity = vector</code></td>
            <td>States how fast the body is travelling; the physics engine performs the motion.</td>
            <td><strong>Yes</strong></td>
            <td>A Rigidbody2D with Body Type <strong>Dynamic</strong>. Movement is in units per <em>second</em>, so no <code>Time.deltaTime</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code></td>
            <td>Sweeps the body to a target position and tests the path as it goes.</td>
            <td>Yes &mdash; <strong>but only on a Dynamic body</strong></td>
            <td>A Rigidbody2D with Body Type <strong>Dynamic</strong>. The docs recommend Kinematic, and that is the one body type where it does <em>not</em> work.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code></td>
            <td>Applies a physical push; mass, drag and inertia all apply.</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>A Dynamic Rigidbody2D with a mass you will have to tune.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>The documentation recommends the one combination that does not work.</strong>
        Read this carefully, because it is a lesson about reading documentation and not a
        trick. Unity's page for <code>MovePosition</code> ends with the note
        <em>"MovePosition is intended for use with kinematic rigidbodies."</em> But the same
        page also says that <em>"if the rigidbody is kinematic then any collisions won't affect
        the rigidbody itself"</em> — and the manual page on body types says a Kinematic body
        <em>"only collides with Dynamic Rigidbody 2Ds and not the other Body Types."</em>
        <br><br>
        Put together: on a Kinematic body, <code>MovePosition</code> moves you to wherever you
        asked and <strong>nothing stops you on the way</strong>. The recommended pairing is the
        broken one. The rule that survives contact with all of it is simpler:
        <strong>for anything in the world to stop you, your body must be Dynamic.</strong>
        <br><br>
        And that is why this course's answer is the row above it —
        <code>linearVelocity</code>, on a Dynamic body. It needs no fine print.
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Why your coins still work either way.</strong> Triggers are the exception to
        every rule above: a collider with <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked reports the overlap
        to <em>any</em> body type, including Kinematic. So a coin picks up whether you are
        Dynamic or Kinematic, while a wall and a cave door only notice a Dynamic you. This is
        exactly why "it works in my game" is not the same as "it is right."
    </div>

